// TabPlayer's recorded drum kit: the Virtuosity Drums recordings (the mid microphone, and close microphones for the kick and snare), one key per kit sound.
// Keys follow General MIDI, except 1 (snare rim shot) and 2 (half-open hi-hat), which General MIDI lacks.
// Where the recordings sound their layers' dynamics themselves, amp_velcurve_1=1 keeps every layer at full level.
// volume sets each drum sound to the General MIDI kit's loudness (K-weighted, first 300 ms, channel volume 119) at velocity 95, the Guitar Pro default dynamic,
// then puts the kick 2 dB and the snare 3 dB forward and the hi-hats 2 dB and the other cymbals 3 dB back. The other sounds keep their peak levelling at velocity 100.
// Candidate C (candidate B with close microphones): the kick comes from the kick microphone and the snare, its cross stick and its rim shot from the snare
// microphone, instead of the mid microphone the rest of the kit uses. The upstream kit has no microphone close to the toms or the cymbals. The kick,
// closed and open hi-hat, crash and ride list their regions here with a level for each recorded layer, because their layers are 6 to 15 dB apart, more than
// the velocity gain rises across one layer (4 to 7 dB); each layer is trimmed so the sound follows one smooth curve, 24 dB per decade of velocity.
// Candidate D (candidate C with the kick 6 dB louder): every kick region (keys 35 and 36) is 6 dB louder. Measured on candidate C, the kick's energy sat 90% in
// 60 to 120 Hz, and above 100 Hz, which is all a small speaker plays, it was 2.1 dB under the snare and 2.7 dB under the rest of the mix at the kick's onsets
// (General MIDI: 5.8 dB over the snare, 3.5 dB over the rest). +6 dB puts it back to General MIDI's presence above 100 Hz.
// Candidate G (candidate D, close and dry): the toms come from the snare microphone and the hi-hats and cymbals from the overhead microphones, instead of the mid-distance
// microphone, whose sound arrives 3 to 5 ms after the close ones' (1 to 1.7 m farther) and carries more room (the snare 8.7 dB less direct, the hi-hat 3 dB, the low tom 7 dB).
// Each moved sound is levelled again to candidate D's loudness at velocity 95 (the toms through the snare microphone are 10 to 14 dB quieter). The percussion keeps the mid-distance microphone.
// group/off_by: a voice of group G stops the voices whose off_by is G on the same track.

<group> key=35 pitch_keytrack=0
